# Enforce line endings for cross-platform compatibility.
# Shell scripts require LF to execute on Linux/macOS — Windows git on checkout
# will otherwise convert them to CRLF and bash will fail with "bad interpreter".

# Default: auto-detect text, normalize to LF on commit
* text=auto eol=lf

# Shell scripts must be LF (bash cannot parse CRLF line endings)
*.sh        text eol=lf
*.bash      text eol=lf

# PowerShell: LF works fine but CRLF is also accepted; prefer LF for consistency
*.ps1       text eol=lf
*.psm1      text eol=lf
*.cmd       text eol=crlf
*.bat       text eol=crlf

# Python / config files
*.py        text eol=lf
*.yml       text eol=lf
*.yaml      text eol=lf
*.json      text eol=lf
*.toml      text eol=lf
*.md        text eol=lf

# Binary files — never touch
*.png       binary
*.jpg       binary
*.jpeg      binary
*.gif       binary
*.ico       binary
*.pdf       binary
*.zip       binary
*.tar       binary
*.gz        binary
*.7z        binary
*.pt        binary
*.onnx      binary
*.bin       binary

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# v0.2.56: union merge driver for knowledge-graph nodes.
#
# WHY: A real 3rd-party user's Claude COMMITS knowledge-graph nodes locally
# (the encouraged behavior — see CLAUDE.md + the post-file-edit hook). When a
# later update folds in upstream, a KG file the user appended to ALSO touched
# upstream appears on both sides of the merge. Git's default merge marks the
# overlapping append region as a CONFLICT even though both sides are purely
# ADDITIVE (the user appended a node; upstream appended an unrelated one).
#
# `merge=union` is git's BUILT-IN union driver: on conflict it keeps BOTH
# sides' lines (concatenated) instead of writing conflict markers. For
# append-only KG nodes this is exactly right — never loses a local node,
# never loses an upstream addition, never needs a human. It is a no-op when
# there is no conflict (the normal 3-way merge still runs).
#
# `union` is BUILT-IN: it needs NO `git config merge.<x>.driver` registration
# in the user's `.git/config`. (Custom `ours`/`theirs` drivers WOULD require
# that registration — `.gitattributes` alone can't enable a non-builtin
# driver — so we deliberately avoid them.)
#
# SCOPE NOTES (deliberately NARROW — verified during the v0.2.56 review):
#   * Only `knowledge/**/*.md` gets the driver. CLAUDE.md / CLAUDE.local.md /
#     .claude/CONTEXT_STATE.md / .claude/MEMORY.md / HANDOFF-*.md were
#     considered and REJECTED: CLAUDE.md is modify-heavy (in-place edits to
#     ports/paths/rules), where union would keep BOTH contradictory lines —
#     a clean conflict the user resolves is safer there. The other four are
#     gitignored in this repo (git never merges gitignored files), so a rule
#     for them would be a dead no-op.
#   * FORWARD-ONLY: git reads the merge driver from the merge-base/HEAD side,
#     so union does NOT apply to the very update that first introduces this
#     file — it activates on updates AFTER v0.2.56 is committed locally. KG
#     nodes are usually NEW files (which never conflict), so the practical
#     gap is small. NOTE (see the accuracy block on the v0.2.71 rules below):
#     this union driver is consulted ONLY by hand-run `git pull`/`git merge`;
#     the launcher A0 auto-merge path uses `git merge-file`, which ignores
#     `.gitattributes` drivers entirely. So for an OVERLAPPING-append KG node
#     the union driver (hand-run merges) is what folds it; the A0 path folds
#     only the NON-overlapping case and sidecars the overlapping one.
#   * launcher/dist/** binaries intentionally get NO driver — a merge driver
#     could not carry this case anyway (see the take-upstream block below).
#     Three sub-cases exist and they are resolved by DIFFERENT mechanisms:
#       (a) local-unchanged + upstream-changed (binary-download users): the
#           3-way merge folds trivially to upstream. This — and only this —
#           is the case a driver-less merge already handled.
#       (b) uncommitted-dirty dist (a compile-from-source rebuild left the
#           binary modified in the working tree): the autostash POP 3-ways
#           the binary and conflicts → autostash-pop modal.
#       (c) committed-divergent dist (a fork that COMMITS rebuilt binaries):
#           merge-tree conflicts on the binary → FfOnly → divergence modal.
#     (b) and (c) are now resolved to upstream automatically by the
#     GENERATED_RELEASE_CONTROLLED_PATTERNS take-upstream reconcile class in
#     launcher/src-tauri/src/commands/git_user_editable_merge.rs (launcher/dist/**
#     is on that allowlist). See the take-upstream block below for why the
#     mechanism is Rust and not a merge driver.

knowledge/**/*.md          merge=union

# v0.2.71: extend the union driver to .gitignore (and .gitattributes itself).
# SAME append-structured shape as KG nodes: forks routinely APPEND ignore
# rules; upstream periodically APPENDS its own. Git's default merge marks an
# overlapping append region as a conflict even when both sides are purely
# additive. `union` keeps both sides' lines — for ignore-rules a duplicate or
# extra ignore line is HARMLESS (never breaks anything), unlike CLAUDE.md
# (modify-heavy, where union would keep contradictory config lines — correctly
# still NOT union'd).
#
# ACCURACY (corrected by the v0.2.71 adversarial review — an earlier version
# of this comment OVERSTATED the A0 path's reach): there are TWO distinct
# reconciliation paths and they handle the OVERLAPPING-append case
# DIFFERENTLY, so name the split honestly:
#   - Hand-run `git pull` / `git merge` DO consult these `.gitattributes`
#     drivers, so a both-append-at-EOF .gitignore/.gitattributes folds via
#     `union` (keeps both lines, no conflict). FORWARD-ONLY per the caveat
#     above (git reads the driver from the merge-base/HEAD side).
#   - The launcher A0 auto-merge path uses `git merge-file`, a low-level
#     primitive that does NOT consult `.gitattributes` drivers AT ALL. So
#     `union` does NOTHING for the A0 path: a both-append-at-EOF overlap
#     CONFLICTS under merge-file → A0 sidecars the upstream copy + writes a
#     deferral (no data loss, but NOT a silent union fold). A0 only folds the
#     NON-overlapping case (local-at-top + upstream-at-bottom).
# So neither path is unconditionally "primary": for the OVERLAPPING-append
# case the union driver (hand-run merges) is the only one that folds; for the
# NON-overlapping case the launcher A0 path folds and union is redundant. The
# A0 allowlist (now covering .gitignore/.gitattributes) keeps the launcher
# from poisoning the WHOLE auto-merge into the divergence modal on a benign
# non-overlapping edit; the union driver here is the hand-run-merge net.
# Deliberately NOT extended to README.md / docs/**: those CAN have a
# semantically-wrong union (two contradictory prose sentences kept side by
# side reads as broken), so they go through the A0 sidecar-on-conflict path
# (clean-merge folds, real-conflict sidecars + defers) rather than union.
.gitignore                 merge=union
.gitattributes             merge=union

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# v0.2.89: generated / release-controlled files get NO merge driver here — on
# divergence they resolve TO UPSTREAM (take-upstream), the INVERSE bias from
# the union driver above. Covered set: launcher/package.json,
# launcher/package-lock.json, launcher/src-tauri/Cargo.lock, launcher/dist/**.
# These are npm/cargo/build artifacts + the upstream-bumped version field;
# they carry no hand-authored value on an installed instance and upstream
# refreshes them essentially every release, so a divergent fork conflicts on
# EVERY future update — the definition of an "expected conflict".
#
# WHY NOT a `.gitattributes` driver (the same honesty as the v0.2.71 block):
#   1. theirs-bias is NOT a built-in git merge driver. Only `union` is usable
#      without a per-clone `git config merge.<x>.driver` registration in the
#      user's `.git/config` (see the union block above); a custom take-theirs
#      driver would need that registration, which `.gitattributes` alone
#      cannot supply — the same reason we avoid custom `ours`/`theirs` drivers
#      for the KG nodes.
#   2. drivers are FORWARD-ONLY (git reads them from the merge-base/HEAD side),
#      so even a registered driver would not apply to the very update that
#      first ships it — and it would do NOTHING for the committed-divergence
#      case, which needs a synthetic commit to un-diverge history (a
#      worktree/index operation alone cannot).
#   3. the launcher's own reconcile paths are driver-BLIND: A0 uses
#      `git merge-file` and the take-upstream class uses `git checkout
#      <theirs> --`, neither of which consults `.gitattributes` drivers at all
#      (same limitation documented for the A0 path above).
# So the mechanism is the Rust reconcile class
# GENERATED_RELEASE_CONTROLLED_PATTERNS in
# launcher/src-tauri/src/commands/git_user_editable_merge.rs — it handles both
# the committed-divergent case (synthetic take-upstream commit) and the
# working-tree-dirty case (restore to HEAD before the pull takes upstream),
# writes a `generated_files_reconciled` audit deferral, and (unlike a driver)
# is disjoint-by-invariant from the USER_EDITABLE_PATTERNS preserve-local list.
# A divergent SOURCE file (Cargo.toml, tauri.conf.json, pyproject.toml,
# vct-module.json, *.py, *.rs) is deliberately NOT in the take-upstream set —
# a local edit there is a meaningful signal the divergence modal should
# surface, not silently overwrite.
